[Music]  one day I got a call from Evans a pouran  friend of mine and colleague and  composer at MIT and he said that he was  approached by Sanjay Sarma of open  learning to see if he could create some  kind of musical experience for MIT  virtual commencement just to fool around  with it I just kind of wrote something  really quickly just as a basic proof of  concept you know like how about  something like this  [Music]  and send it to Iran he went oh that's  perfect let's just used which never  happens it was really important to me  that we could have every person  contribute and of course this is a  musical project which means that  probably people who are musicians are  going to feel more comfortable  but we really wanted everyone to feel  comfortable contributing and so we did  an analysis of all of the notes in the  piece that Evan wrote and figured out  which ones would be harder to sing but  which ones might be easier to sing and  so on the submissions website anyone can  pick their level of comfort or  experience and then the system  automatically gives them notes to sing  that are appropriate for their comfort  level everyone who submitted out out  there you don't really have to worry  about whether you were singing exactly  in tune we kind of fixed it for you  [Music]  [Music]  okay  hey everyone getting the team together  to make it happen was essential and was  quintessentially MIT because I think MIT  is one of the few places where you think  of a project like this and you go okay  we're gonna need you know a composer  working with Joel Ralston we're gonna  need fantastic audio people we're gonna  need the most amazing coder on the  planet they're gonna be fantastic web  developers  you're gonna need fantastic video people  you don't need fantastic communications  people to get the word out and all these  people are a phone call away or an email  away and will answer and everybody is  devoted to MIT theses are still coming  on I still remember the first meeting  when we started to get people together  and we're talking about the website that  we would need to get input from  participants and we're all going oh god  that sounds so complicated that's gonna  take you know two weeks just to get that  up and hike just very softly says I'll  have it ready by 2:00 p.m. tomorrow and  then he did one of the neat things is we  had this feedback box and we intended  this to be like this piece of the  website did not work well but actually  instead in the feedback box we started  getting these comments like hey man this  is awesome  way to go Iran hooray for MIT it was  incredible to see that people really did  contribute  you could see every minute or so every  30 seconds or so at the peak it would be  another clip showing up one of the  really wonderful things I noticed was  that people were being creative with how  they submitted and we also saw a lot of  families coming together and it was  really wonderful to see a sense of  community even when we were just asking  for a single dope  if this were just a song that was sung  it wouldn't be quite as interesting as  challenging really fascinating part of  this project has been the effort of  putting the video together with the  music the visuals for the piece were  left really open-ended which was  wonderful and the idea of sort of a  journey came to mind the idea of sort of  creating this journey that the student  takes every day as part of this piece  really I thought resonated with this  idea of students going through their  their commencement and through their  actual academic journey there's a part  of the piece that I wrote where the  music kind of ascends when I wrote it I  wasn't thinking of anything it was just  something I felt it was needed basically  at that time but what was really magic  to me was what Clayton first showed us  that moment the camera crosses NASA  Abbott would walk up the stairs into the  infinite corridor  [Music]  and it matches the music absolutely  perfectly and it made the music work it  got you know it was the magical moment  for me that's the real sign to me of a  great collaboration when you do what you  do as well as you can and then somebody  else does what they do and they take  what you've done and turn it into  something bigger than what you thought  it could be there's a discussion early  on about trying to hide some some MIT  Easter eggs inside the piece at the time  I was thinking you know it'd be good  just to get the piece done let's worry  about you strikes later on but we did  put a four different moments inside the  piece and we're excited that people  might find them and share those I think  the the most heartwarming part of this  project to me is the fact that it really  is the voices of the graduates of 2020  and these are MIT students who have put  their heart and their expression into  this musical piece and therefore this is  really a creation of the students by the  students and for the students  [Music]

